#
# RadiANT heart-rate strap.
#
# One prefix for the whole application, STRAP_*, the same way the dongle uses
# ANT_DONGLE_* and the simulator uses ANT_SIM_*. The three never build together,
# but a shared prefix would make "which application does this symbol belong to"
# ambiguous, and the answer to that question is how the CI assertions stay
# honest.
#

rsource "../../radiant/src/node/Kconfig"
rsource "../../radiant/src/profiles/Kconfig"
rsource "../common/Kconfig.antr_api"

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

menuconfig STRAP
	bool "RadiANT heart-rate strap"
	default y
	help
	  A RadiANT NODE: radiant driving a master channel with
	  src/profiles/profile_hr.c on top, broadcasting ANT+ heart rate
	  (device type 0x78). No USB and no serial bridge - this is not a
	  dongle, and the only host interface is the log.

	  It exists for P8 of the multiprotocol plan: the BLE coexistence work
	  needs a node whose ANT+ side is a master, because a master owns its
	  slot phase and a slave does not, and what happens to a transmit
	  schedule under an arbiter is the whole question.

if STRAP

config STRAP_DEVICE_NUMBER
	int "ANT device number when the node is not provisioned"
	range 1 65535
	default 4242
	help
	  Used only when src/node/ has no identity record. A provisioned node
	  takes its device number from that record instead, which is what
	  ADR 0009 requires - the number is a property of the node's identity,
	  not of the image. The fallback keeps a freshly-flashed DK usable on a
	  bench without a provisioning step.

config STRAP_HR_BPM
	int "simulated heart rate, beats per minute"
	range 30 220
	default 72
	help
	  A DK has no electrodes. The beat generator produces beats at this
	  rate so the page 0 event count and event time - the fields that
	  actually survive a lost packet - advance the way a real strap's do.

config STRAP_MANUFACTURER_ID
	int "manufacturer id for the common pages"
	range 0 255
	default 255
	help
	  255 is the ANT+ development id. A shipped strap needs a real one from
	  the alliance; using it here would be claiming somebody else's.

config STRAP_BLE_HRS
	bool "also advertise the SIG Heart Rate Service over BLE"
	depends on BT && BT_PERIPHERAL
	select BT_HRS
	help
	  P8b: the SoftDevice Controller beside the ANT+ master, advertising
	  the standard SIG Heart Rate Service, so that one strap serves both a
	  phone and an ANT+ receiver.

	  THE CONNECTION AND ADVERTISING INTERVALS ARE THE PRIORITY MECHANISM,
	  not a power tuning. Finding 1 of the plan: MPSL's two
	  application-visible priorities rank us against ourselves and not
	  against the Bluetooth controller, which outranks anything an
	  application can request. So RadiANT keeps its slots by SHAPING the
	  other stack's demand, exactly as nrf/samples/esb/esb_ptx_ble does
	  with a 1 s connection interval.

config STRAP_BLE_ADV_INTERVAL_MS
	int "BLE advertising interval, milliseconds"
	depends on STRAP_BLE_HRS
	range 100 10000
	default 1000
	help
	  1-2 s is what the plan specifies for the strap. Shortening it is not
	  a power trade, it is a decision to take air away from ANT+, and the
	  coexistence gate is what says whether it can be afforded.

config STRAP_BLE_MIN_CONN_INTERVAL_UNITS
	int "shortest connection interval to accept, 1.25 ms units"
	depends on STRAP_BLE_HRS
	range 6 3200
	default 800
	help
	  800 units is 1 s, which is what nrf/samples/esb/esb_ptx_ble uses to
	  run BLE beside a timeslot user, and it is chosen for the same reason.
	  A phone will ask for far less - iOS asks for tens of milliseconds -
	  and accepting hands the controller a recurring high-priority event
	  every few milliseconds for the life of the connection. MPSL gives the
	  controller priority levels above anything this application can
	  request, so that air is simply gone; refusing the update is the only
	  place the decision can be made.

config STRAP_SUPPRESS_ANT_WHEN_CONNECTED
	bool "stop ANT+ while a BLE connection is live"
	depends on STRAP_BLE_HRS
	default y
	help
	  P9's suppression rule, as policy above the link layer: a phone that
	  has connected is receiving the same heart rate over the SIG service,
	  so continuing to broadcast ANT+ spends air on a duplicate - and it
	  means the hard contention case never normally arises at all.

	  Turn it off for the minority-manufacturer behaviour, where straps
	  keep transmitting ANT+ while connected so a watch and a phone can be
	  served at once. That configuration is the one the coexistence gate
	  actually stresses.

module = STRAP
module-str = strap
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # STRAP
